==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|ratte|lang=gmh}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|ratta|lang=goh}}. There is still some uncertainty about the origin of the word and that of the animal itself, which was unknown to Europe in antiquity. Older etymological sources explain the [[Germanic]] words as borrowings from [[Romance]]. It is now widely accepted that this is not the case. The further etymology is unsettled. The word may go back to an unknown [[substrate]] language, or be formed from a [[Proto-Indo-European]] root meaning „to gnaw“ (compare {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|rōdere|lang=la}}). The consonantism ''ratta'' in Old High German (instead of *''razza''), along with other hints, makes it likely that the former is borrowed from {{etyl|osx|de}} {{term|ratta|lang=osx}}. The dialectal German variant {{term|Ratz|lang=de}} is probably an expressive derivative, though it could be an Old High German adaptation of the Old Saxon form. See {{etyl|en|-}} {{term|rat|lang=en}} for more. Compare also {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|rat|lang=de}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|råtta|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|fr|-}} {{term|rat|lang=fr}}.

